steps.body:
A strategy filters ticks with small rules like "the spread is wider than 1.5 cents", and the natural way to write one is a lambda that captures the threshold. Knowing what a lambda is in memory tells you what copying it costs, what `std::function` has to store later in this lesson, and why templates make lambdas free.

The compiler turns every lambda into an unnamed struct, its *closure type*, with a `const` `operator()`. Each value capture becomes a data member, copied in when the lambda is made. These two are the same thing:

```cpp harness
struct Tick {
    int64_t ts_ns;
    double bid, ask;
    double bid_sz, ask_sz;
};
const double max_spread = 0.015;
{{code}}
const Tick t{0, 101.25, 101.27, 5, 3};
std::cout << std::boolalpha << wider(t) << ' ' << WiderThan{max_spread}(t) << '\n';
std::cout << sizeof(wider) << ' ' << sizeof(WiderThan) << '\n';
```

```cpp
auto wider = [max_spread](const Tick& t) { return t.ask - t.bid > max_spread; };

struct WiderThan {
    double max_spread;
    bool operator()(const Tick& t) const { return t.ask - t.bid > max_spread; }
};
```

So `sizeof` a lambda is the size of its captures, with the usual padding. These sizes are the same with g++ here and with clang on a Mac:

| Capture list | sizeof | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `[]` | 1 | an empty struct still takes a byte |
| `[max_spread]` | 8 | one `double` |
| `[max_spread, min_size]` | 16 | a `double` and an `int`, padded to 16 |
| `[&max_spread]` | 8 | a reference capture is stored as a pointer |
| `[ticks]` | 24 | a whole `std::vector<Tick>`, copied with every tick in it |

Every lambda expression also has a type of its own, even two with the same text, so `std::is_same_v<decltype(a), decltype(b)>` is `false`. When you pass a lambda to a template such as `std::count_if`, the compiler stamps out a copy of the template for that exact closure type. Inside it, the call goes to one known `operator()`, so the optimizer can inline it, just as it inlines `price(Call{}, S, K)` from *Compile-Time C++: constexpr, static_assert, and Type Traits*.

example.code:
#include <algorithm>
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>
#include <type_traits>
#include <vector>

struct Tick {
    int64_t ts_ns;          // nanoseconds since midnight
    double bid, ask;        // prices
    double bid_sz, ask_sz;  // sizes
};

struct WiderThan {
    double max_spread;
    bool operator()(const Tick& t) const { return t.ask - t.bid > max_spread; }
};

int main() {
    const std::vector<Tick> ticks = {{34200000000000, 101.25, 101.27, 3, 5},
                                     {34200000250000, 101.26, 101.27, 2, 4},
                                     {34200000900000, 101.24, 101.28, 7, 1},
                                     {34200001400000, 101.25, 101.26, 6, 6}};
    const double max_spread = 0.015;
    const int min_size = 5;

    auto spread = [](const Tick& t) { return t.ask - t.bid; };
    auto wide = [max_spread](const Tick& t) { return t.ask - t.bid > max_spread; };
    auto wide_deep = [max_spread, min_size](const Tick& t) {
        return t.ask - t.bid > max_spread && t.bid_sz >= min_size;
    };
    auto wide_ref = [&max_spread](const Tick& t) { return t.ask - t.bid > max_spread; };
    auto wide_again = [max_spread](const Tick& t) { return t.ask - t.bid > max_spread; };

    std::cout << "sizeof: " << sizeof(spread) << ' ' << sizeof(wide) << ' ' << sizeof(wide_deep)
              << ' ' << sizeof(wide_ref) << ' ' << sizeof(WiderThan) << '\n';
    std::cout << std::boolalpha << "same type: "
              << std::is_same_v<decltype(wide), decltype(wide_again)> << '\n';
    std::cout << "wide: " << std::count_if(ticks.begin(), ticks.end(), wide) << '\n';
    std::cout << "WiderThan: "
              << std::count_if(ticks.begin(), ticks.end(), WiderThan{max_spread}) << '\n';
    std::cout << "wide and deep: " << std::count_if(ticks.begin(), ticks.end(), wide_deep) << '\n';
    std::cout << "first spread: " << spread(ticks[0]) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

