steps.body:
A market-data handler keeps its subscribers in a `std::vector<std::function<void(const Tick&)>>`: a risk check, a signal, a logger, each registered at start-up and called on every tick. One `std::function` type can hold all of them because it hides each callable's real type behind a fixed interface, which is called **type erasure**. `std::any` from `<any>` erases the type of a single value the same way, for any copyable type, and hands it back through `std::any_cast<T>`. In g++'s library you can see how in its 32 bytes:

- 16 bytes of storage for the callable,
- a pointer to a *manager* function that copies and destroys it, null when the `std::function` is empty (the `cmp QWORD PTR [rdi+16], 0` in the previous part),
- a pointer to an *invoker* that calls it, the `[rdi+24]` in the assembly of the previous part.

A callable that is trivially copyable and fits in 16 bytes is stored inside. Anything bigger, or anything with a real copy constructor such as a `std::string` capture, is created with `new` on the heap, and each copy of the `std::function` makes another heap copy.

To count those, replace the global `operator new`, as *Errors in Fast Code: noexcept, Error Codes, and Results* and *`string_view` and Fast Parsing* did: every `new` in the program calls it, including the ones inside the standard library. *Allocations: reserve, Small Buffers, and Hot Paths*, later in this section, covers it in full. This is the minimal version, with a matching `operator delete`:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

{{code}}

int main() {
    const double a = 1.0;
    const double b = 2.0;
    const double c = 3.0;
    const long before = g_allocs;
    std::function<double(double)> f = [a, b, c](double x) { return a * x * x + b * x + c; };
    std::cout << g_allocs - before << " allocation, f(2) = " << f(2.0) << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
long g_allocs = 0;

// Every new in the program calls this, including those inside the standard library.
void* operator new(std::size_t n) {
    ++g_allocs;
    void* p = std::malloc(n);
    if (p == nullptr) {
        throw std::bad_alloc{};
    }
    return p;
}
void operator delete(void* p) noexcept { std::free(p); }
void operator delete(void* p, std::size_t) noexcept { std::free(p); }
```

The Mac's libc++ makes `std::function` 32 bytes as well, and it keeps a callable inside when it fits in 16 bytes and its copy can't throw. So the allocation counts below come out the same on a Mac, though the byte totals differ a little and the two libraries' rules part ways in corner cases.

To keep a hot path free of allocations: build every `std::function` at start-up, not per tick, capture little (a reference to a config struct you keep alive, through `[&config]`, or `std::ref(config)` from `<functional>`, which wraps the reference in an 8-byte object), move a `std::function` instead of copying it, or take a template parameter instead.

example.code:
#include <algorithm>
#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <functional>
#include <iostream>
#include <new>
#include <string>
#include <utility>

long g_allocs = 0;
std::size_t g_bytes = 0;

// Every new in the program calls this, including those inside the standard library.
void* operator new(std::size_t n) {
    ++g_allocs;
    g_bytes += n;
    void* p = std::malloc(n);
    if (p == nullptr) {
        throw std::bad_alloc{};
    }
    return p;
}
void operator delete(void* p) noexcept { std::free(p); }
void operator delete(void* p, std::size_t) noexcept { std::free(p); }

struct Call {
    double operator()(double S, double K) const { return std::max(S - K, 0.0); }
};

using PayoffFn = std::function<double(double, double)>;

long mark = 0;
std::size_t mark_bytes = 0;
void report(const char* what) {
    std::cout << what << ": allocs " << g_allocs - mark << ", bytes " << g_bytes - mark_bytes << '\n';
    mark = g_allocs;
    mark_bytes = g_bytes;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << "sizeof(PayoffFn) = " << sizeof(PayoffFn) << '\n';
    const double lo = 1.0;
    const double hi = 20.0;
    const double notional = 1000.0;
    const std::string book = "rates";
    mark = g_allocs;
    mark_bytes = g_bytes;

    PayoffFn call = Call{};
    report("Call{}");
    PayoffFn capped = [hi](double S, double K) { return std::min(std::max(S - K, 0.0), hi); };
    report("8 bytes of captures");
    PayoffFn collar = [lo, hi](double S, double K) { return std::clamp(S - K, lo, hi); };
    report("16 bytes of captures");
    PayoffFn scaled = [lo, hi, notional](double S, double K) {
        return notional * std::clamp(S - K, lo, hi);
    };
    report("24 bytes of captures");
    PayoffFn copy = scaled;
    report("a copy of that");
    PayoffFn moved = std::move(scaled);
    report("a move of that");
    PayoffFn tagged = [book](double S, double K) { return book.size() * std::max(S - K, 0.0); };
    report("a std::string capture");
    std::cout << call(105, 100) << ' ' << capped(150, 100) << ' ' << collar(100.5, 100) << ' '
              << copy(105, 100) << ' ' << moved(105, 100) << ' ' << tagged(105, 100) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

