steps.body:
An order gateway handles exactly three kinds of order, market, limit, and stop, and no plugin will add a fourth at run time. For a closed set like that, a class hierarchy in a `std::vector<std::unique_ptr<OrderBase>>` costs a heap allocation per order and a pointer to follow on every access. `std::variant<MarketOrder, LimitOrder, StopOrder>` holds one of the three *inside itself*, plus a small index, as *Errors in Fast Code: noexcept, Error Codes, and Results* showed when it built `Result`. So a vector of orders is one contiguous block:

```cpp
struct MarketOrder { int64_t qty; };
struct LimitOrder { int64_t qty; double price; };
struct StopOrder { int64_t qty; double trigger; };
using Order = std::variant<MarketOrder, LimitOrder, StopOrder>;
std::cout << sizeof(Order) << '\n';  // 24: the largest, 16, plus the index, padded
```

That lesson read a variant one type at a time, with `index()` and `std::get_if`. For a closed set, `std::visit(visitor, order)` handles every type at once: it calls the visitor's `operator()` overload for whichever type the order holds. The visitor is a function object with one overload per alternative, and leaving one out is a compile error, so the compiler checks that every kind is handled.

What it compiles to, with g++ 10.2: `std::visit` calls through a table of function pointers, one per alternative, so it is an indirect call much like a virtual one (the Mac's libc++ does the same). A chain of `std::get_if` compiles to compares on the index byte, with everything inlined:

```text
notional_visit:                         # std::visit(Notional{last}, o)
        movzx   eax, BYTE PTR [rdi+16]  # the index
        ...
        call    [QWORD PTR _ZNSt8__detail9__variant12__gen_vtable...[0+rax*8]]
notional_get_if:                        # the same with std::get_if
        movzx   eax, BYTE PTR [rdi+16]
        cmp     al, 1                   # a LimitOrder?
        jne     .L8
        ...
        cvtsi2sd        xmm0, QWORD PTR [rdi]   # qty
        mulsd   xmm0, QWORD PTR [rdi+8]         # * price
```

The table's long name is cut short above. So the big win of `std::variant` is memory, which the example counts. Reach for `std::get_if` when the dispatch itself is the hot spot.

The other static tool is **CRTP**, the curiously recurring template pattern: a base class template that takes the derived class as its parameter, so it can call the derived class's function directly, with no vtable. The call goes through `static_cast<Derived*>(this)`, the unchecked downcast from *Conversions and Casts*, safe here because a `StrategyBase<BidPressure>` is always part of a `BidPressure`. It suits a strategy base that does shared bookkeeping and calls each strategy's `signal`:

```cpp harness
struct Tick {
    int64_t ts_ns;
    double bid, ask;
    double bid_sz, ask_sz;
};
{{code}}
BidPressure s;
s.on_tick(Tick{0, 101.25, 101.27, 9, 3});
s.on_tick(Tick{1, 101.25, 101.27, 8, 2});
std::cout << s.position() << '\n';
```

```cpp
template <typename Derived>
class StrategyBase {
public:
    void on_tick(const Tick& t) { position_ += static_cast<Derived*>(this)->signal(t); }
    int position() const { return position_; }

private:
    int position_ = 0;
};

class BidPressure : public StrategyBase<BidPressure> {
public:
    int signal(const Tick& t) const { return t.bid_sz > 2 * t.ask_sz ? 1 : 0; }
};
```

`StrategyBase<BidPressure>` knows at compile time that `this` is a `BidPressure`. For a function that calls `s.on_tick(t)`, g++ 10.2 at `-O2` inlines `signal` and leaves no call and not even a branch:

```text
        movsd   xmm0, QWORD PTR [rsi+32]   # ask_sz
        movsd   xmm1, QWORD PTR [rsi+24]   # bid_sz
        xor     eax, eax
        addsd   xmm0, xmm0                 # 2 * ask_sz
        comisd  xmm1, xmm0
        seta    al                         # 1 if bid_sz is bigger
        add     DWORD PTR [rdi], eax       # position_ += signal
        ret
```

The price is that `StrategyBase<BidPressure>` and `StrategyBase<Momentum>` are unrelated types, so they can't share one container. When you need that, it's a virtual call or a variant again.

